%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 8ba5544929d569a4a808ebb5b67f1847, type: 3}
  m_Name: GuidedNarrationCatalogue
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  entries:
  - id: welcome
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: b1411d8f140636e489bd1ecef8759e42, type: 3}
    subtitle: Welcome to the Virtual Physics Lab. Today, we will explore Hooke's
      Law and discover how a spring stretches when we add weight. Look around, then
      use point teleportation to move towards the experiment table.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 1
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 14.28
  - id: movement
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: a07e2c3576f175149a17b15e220dfa4d, type: 3}
    subtitle: Point your thumbstick forward toward the floor and release when the
      teleport path turns blue-cyan to move to the experiment table.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 8.82
  - id: apparatus
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: cb0310f51b3034c46b4e37d12aa1135f, type: 3}
    subtitle: This is the spring experiment. You can attach different masses, measure
      the spring's extension, and record your results. Select Earth's gravity to
      begin.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 9.66
  - id: hookes_law
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: f86cccffc6fa41745b34ceb951eeb2f9, type: 3}
    subtitle: 'Hooke''s Law states that a spring''s restoring force is proportional
      to its extension within its elastic limit: F equals minus k x.'
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 9.24
  - id: pickup_prompt
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 9de442af68dd72a41adc5352eed0be50, type: 3}
    subtitle: Pick up a weight using the grip button on your controller. Start with
      the smallest available mass.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.14
  - id: pickup_success
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 9d68d894a2b6cd9469ef6eda7188a667, type: 3}
    subtitle: Move the weight towards the spring's attachment point. Release it when
      it is inside the attachment area.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.14
  - id: attach_prompt
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 0eb76930dc62b0c4ca5ad3b5e7787f3b, type: 3}
    subtitle: The weight is inside the attachment area. Release the grip button to
      attach it to the spring.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 3
    minCooldownSeconds: 2.5
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.14
  - id: attach_success
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 31b60e5a451adb041a87ead427339fa1, type: 3}
    subtitle: Well done. The weight pulls the spring down. Wait for the spring to
      settle before taking a measurement.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.56
  - id: return_to_table
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: ab1057cd444661541987a3443a0f38b5, type: 3}
    subtitle: The weight returned to the table. Pick it up again using the grip button,
      hold it in the attachment area, and release.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 3
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 9.24
  - id: equilibrium
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: a5263c7a72f7dfa4d845654ab3f2b854, type: 3}
    subtitle: Wait for the oscillation to settle, or select Settle Spring for static
      equilibrium.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 5.46
  - id: record_prompt
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 0635e7d7cb6f3844e94b2777a607309d, type: 3}
    subtitle: Now observe the extension reading. Extension is the increase in length
      from the spring's unloaded position. Select Record Point to save your measurement.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 9.66
  - id: record_success
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 6ad1e050a4b5c7e4d802a23954d1b3a0, type: 3}
    subtitle: Your measurement has been recorded. Detach the weight to return it
      to the table, then try a different mass. Keep the gravity and spring stiffness
      unchanged while comparing your results.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 12.599999
  - id: record_rejected_moving
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 2f0b9499e99aa924a89607844ed07c95, type: 3}
    subtitle: Recording failed because the spring is still oscillating. Wait for
      equilibrium or select Settle Spring, then record again.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 3
    minCooldownSeconds: 2.5
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.56
  - id: record_rejected_empty
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 11e2916ea928c6140a4b67d722300a09, type: 3}
    subtitle: Recording failed because no mass is attached. Attach a weight and let
      the spring settle before recording.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 3
    minCooldownSeconds: 2.5
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.14
  - id: try_different_loads
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 27443849e41a75541b0026bd72c9cca7, type: 3}
    subtitle: Detach the current weight, attach another mass, and record at least
      three different total masses at the same gravity and spring stiffness.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 9.24
  - id: graph_explanation
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 68bb519f0386eb44eb017d94d7f21593, type: 3}
    subtitle: Look at the force-extension graph. Within the elastic limit, greater
      force produces greater extension. For force plotted vertically against extension
      horizontally, the slope represents the spring constant.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 11.339999
  - id: gravity_prompt
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 43246695f824451478fd8528bcccd688, type: 3}
    subtitle: Now keep the same mass attached and select another planet. Compare
      how the spring's extension changes with gravity.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.56
  - id: gravity_earth
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 6bc8c906a8e2b2b428b42e62a4675f3a, type: 3}
    subtitle: Earth's gravity is selected. Observe the spring's extension.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 2
    minCooldownSeconds: 1.5
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 3.36
  - id: gravity_moon
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 665e2b618446d874fa9b885148c328da, type: 3}
    subtitle: The Moon has weaker gravity than Earth. With the same mass and spring
      stiffness, the equilibrium extension is smaller.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 2
    minCooldownSeconds: 1.5
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.9799995
  - id: gravity_mars
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 5081158fa5b24d842aad5d6a55182acb, type: 3}
    subtitle: Mars has weaker gravity than Earth. With the same mass and spring stiffness,
      the equilibrium extension is smaller.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 2
    minCooldownSeconds: 1.5
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.56
  - id: gravity_jupiter
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 38466c7dbd5d46a4783e6469b751c202, type: 3}
    subtitle: Jupiter has stronger gravity than Earth. With the same mass and spring
      stiffness, the predicted extension is larger, provided the spring remains within
      its elastic range.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 2
    minCooldownSeconds: 1.5
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 10.92
  - id: elastic_limit
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: d8400c1de14890c49b12ae7decceb5fd, type: 3}
    subtitle: The extension has exceeded the experiment's elastic-limit threshold.
      Reduce the load so the spring stays within its elastic range.
    language: en
    priority: 3
    replayRule: 3
    minCooldownSeconds: 5
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 7.9799995
  - id: teacher_help
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: cb83bd4190c58f74bbdeb22b6e324d39, type: 3}
    subtitle: You can now visit the virtual teacher to ask questions about Hooke's
      Law. You can also read the theory board and select YouTube to watch the linked
      educational video.
    language: en
    priority: 1
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 12.179999
  - id: completion
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: a730c867bab0b0a41a63bccf4c36a67b, type: 3}
    subtitle: You have completed the guided experiment. You explored how mass and
      gravity affect spring extension and recorded your results. You can continue
      experimenting and exploring the laboratory.
    language: en
    priority: 2
    replayRule: 0
    minCooldownSeconds: 3
    fallbackDurationSeconds: 11.339999
